The owner’s introductory movie for Screens 01–03 and ending movie for Screens 09–11 were not found. The expected game locations are `public/video/intro.mp4` or `intro.webm` and `public/video/ending.mp4` or `ending.webm`. The lead contains `public/video/PUT_FLOW_VIDEOS_HERE.txt` and the project contains Flow handoff instructions and test captures. The older files named `qa/recordings/screen_01.mp4` through the nonplayable ending screens are documented Chromium/WebGL game captures. The saved `scripts/record-screens.mjs` identifies their origin. The search covered current and historical Git tree filenames, saved workspaces, known project archives, available GitHub Releases and Actions artifacts, and the project’s linked research assets. No original master was identifiable in those sources. The connected environment does not contain the owner’s Google Flow project or phone storage.

Recover the original-quality exports from the owner’s Google Flow project, phone, Google Photos or personal Drive if the owner saved them there. Preserve the masters separately. Use the filenames in the game’s existing Flow instructions when the owner supplies them. Game recordings and cinematic-fallback captures are labeled separately.

## Android signing and distribution

The backup contains the available saved debug APKs and the native project. No saved release AAB was identified. The newly built verification APK is documented in `Verification/build-test.json`.

Private signing files, passwords and signing-service credentials are excluded. A disposable debug keystore existed in the earlier Android test workspace. Android build tools can generate a new debug key. A release or Play upload key must be backed up separately in owner-controlled encrypted storage, together with its alias and separately managed passwords. If the app is already on Google Play, check the Play Console app-signing and upload-key records before shipping an update.

